# 15. /etc/passwd

One of the most important files related to users is:

```text
/etc/passwd
```

It contains information about user accounts.

You can view it using:

```bash
cat /etc/passwd
```

A typical entry looks similar to:

```text
rahul:x:1001:1001:Rahul:/home/rahul:/bin/bash
```

The fields are separated by `:`.

---

# 16. Understanding /etc/passwd

Example:

```text
rahul:x:1001:1001:Rahul:/home/rahul:/bin/bash
```

The fields represent:

```text
Username
Password placeholder
UID
GID
User information
Home directory
Login shell
```

So:

```text
rahul
```

is the username.

```text
1001
```

is the UID.

```text
1001
```

is the GID.

```text
/home/rahul
```

is the home directory.

```text
/bin/bash
```

is the login shell.

---

# 17. Why Is /etc/passwd Important?

The `/etc/passwd` file is important for user management and Linux administration.

It can help you determine:

* Which users exist.
* Their UIDs.
* Their GIDs.
* Their home directories.
* Their login shells.

### Real-World Example

During security investigation, an administrator can inspect `/etc/passwd` to identify user accounts present on a system.

---

# 18. /etc/shadow

Another important Linux file is:

```text
/etc/shadow
```

It contains password-related authentication information.

Because this file contains sensitive authentication data, access is normally restricted.

Check its permissions using:

```bash
ls -l /etc/shadow
```

Do not modify this file manually unless you know exactly what you are doing.

